VALUES ('0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 'secplus-practice-10', 'CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701) Practice Paper 10', 'comptia-sec-plus', ARRAY['CIA Triad', 'Risk Strategies', 'XSS', 'Session Hijacking', 'Zero Trust Pillars', 'STIX/TAXII', 'MITRE ATT&CK', 'NIST CSF', 'COPPA']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 10, 30);

-- ── Domain: General Security Concepts (12% — 2 questions) ──

VALUES ('e4045066-22ed-4a0f-b129-f8723fee7f64', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 1, 'A security team explains the three foundational goals nearly every security control ultimately serves: preventing unauthorized parties from reading sensitive data, ensuring data is not improperly altered, and ensuring authorized users can actually access systems and data when they legitimately need to. What is this foundational three-part model called, and what does each part specifically protect against?');
VALUES ('e4045066-22ed-4a0f-b129-f8723fee7f64', '7f03f633-773f-44bd-b4cb-9a99b7e992b0', 'The CIA triad is the single most foundational model in information security, and essentially every other specific concept covered across this entire project''s Security+ content genuinely maps back to serving at least one of its three goals: Confidentiality is about preventing unauthorized DISCLOSURE — keeping sensitive data readable only by those actually authorized to see it, served by encryption, access control, and data classification (all covered generally in prior papers). Integrity is about preventing unauthorized or accidental MODIFICATION — ensuring data genuinely remains what it''s supposed to be, served by hashing and digital signatures (both covered generally in prior papers), and directly illustrated by the classic "unauthorized database modification" scenario this triad element is built around. Availability is about ensuring legitimate, authorized ACCESS when actually needed — served by redundancy, DDoS mitigation, and backup/recovery planning (all covered generally in prior papers) — a system perfectly confidential and perfectly maintaining integrity but completely unavailable to its legitimate users has still failed a real, core security goal. This is genuinely distinct from the AAA framework (covered generally in a prior paper — Authentication, Authorization, Accounting, an access-control-specific framework, not this broader confidentiality/integrity/availability triad, despite the coincidental shared "three letters" shape, explicitly noted as a common point of confusion when AAA was first covered) — security controls absolutely DO serve this shared, foundational, and genuinely unifying set of goals, not entirely unrelated, standalone purposes.');

VALUES ('85aa63cc-fc29-4920-be92-967deb9d98e5', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 2, 'A risk management team evaluates four distinct strategic responses to an identified risk: choosing not to engage in the risky activity at all, purchasing insurance to shift the financial consequence to a third party, implementing a specific control to reduce the risk''s likelihood or impact, or formally acknowledging the risk and choosing to proceed without further action. What are these four strategies called collectively, and how do they relate to risk acceptance and risk appetite (both covered generally in prior papers)?');
VALUES ('85aa63cc-fc29-4920-be92-967deb9d98e5', '2207e39c-6f04-47dc-b2a7-c364915bfe47', 'These four risk management strategies form the complete, standard STRATEGIC decision space a risk response genuinely chooses from, directly unifying several individual concepts covered separately across prior papers into one coherent framework: avoidance means eliminating the risk entirely by simply not engaging in the risky activity or process at all — the most complete response, but not always practically or commercially viable. Transference shifts the financial CONSEQUENCE of the risk to a third party without actually reducing the risk''s likelihood — cyber insurance (covered generally in a prior paper) is the classic example, though the underlying risk of the incident occurring is unchanged, only who bears the financial cost shifts. Mitigation implements a specific control to genuinely reduce the risk''s likelihood or impact (the vast majority of specific security controls covered throughout this project''s content ultimately serve this mitigation function) without necessarily eliminating the underlying risk to zero. And acceptance (covered generally in a prior paper) means formally acknowledging the risk and proceeding without further action — ideally a DELIBERATE decision made consistently within the organization''s pre-established risk appetite (also covered generally in a prior paper), rather than an ad hoc, uninformed choice. Every real risk decision genuinely selects among these four distinct strategic options based on cost, feasibility, and risk tolerance — not a single, undifferentiated response applied uniformly regardless of the specific risk''s actual characteristics.');

-- ── Domain: Threats, Vulnerabilities & Mitigations (22% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('4b70439a-b5b2-4ea6-85ec-ab4a8e858753', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 3, 'A vulnerable web forum allows a user to post a comment containing a malicious script, which then gets PERMANENTLY STORED in the forum''s database and automatically executes in the browser of EVERY subsequent visitor who views that comment — distinct from a different vulnerability where a malicious script is embedded directly in a crafted URL and only executes for the single victim who clicks that specific malicious link. What is this class of vulnerability called, and what are these two distinct variants?');
VALUES ('4b70439a-b5b2-4ea6-85ec-ab4a8e858753', '98d7be55-462c-437a-877f-54876fb82fcb', 'XSS is specifically defined by causing an attacker''s malicious SCRIPT to actually EXECUTE within another user''s browser, genuinely distinct in mechanism from CSRF (covered generally in a prior paper), which instead forges a REQUEST leveraging a victim''s already-authenticated session without injecting or executing any new code at all — the CSRF-versus-XSS distinction was noted specifically when CSRF was originally covered, and this question now details XSS''s own two major variants. Stored XSS is the more severe of the two specifically because the malicious script gets PERMANENTLY saved server-side (in a database backing a comment section, forum post, or similar user-generated content), meaning it automatically executes against EVERY subsequent visitor who simply views that stored content, with zero further action needed from the attacker after the initial injection — a genuinely wide-reaching, "set it and forget it" attack. Reflected XSS instead requires the malicious script to be embedded directly within a specifically crafted request (most commonly a URL), meaning it only actually executes for the ONE specific victim who''s individually tricked into clicking that particular malicious link — a narrower attack requiring the attacker to actively distribute the crafted link to each intended victim, rather than the vulnerability doing the work automatically once. This is a real, well-documented, and historically extremely significant vulnerability class specifically because browsers genuinely do execute script content embedded within a page''s content by design, which is exactly the underlying behavior XSS exploits when an application fails to properly sanitize user-supplied input before rendering it back to other users.');

VALUES ('a7ddbbb1-17ff-4c59-a65c-12f88aefd62d', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 4, 'An attacker steals a legitimate, already-authenticated user''s session token (or session cookie) — for example, by intercepting unencrypted traffic or via a successful XSS attack (covered earlier this paper) — and then uses that stolen token directly to impersonate the victim''s active session, gaining access WITHOUT ever needing the victim''s actual username or password at all. What is this specific attack called?');
VALUES ('a7ddbbb1-17ff-4c59-a65c-12f88aefd62d', '11844c1b-fdc1-41e0-a994-b75dbf9ff5db', 'Session hijacking is specifically distinguished by targeting the SESSION TOKEN itself — the piece of data an application actually uses to recognize "this specific request belongs to an already-authenticated user" — rather than targeting the underlying credentials (username/password) at all, which is exactly why it genuinely bypasses the need for a victim''s actual password entirely, distinct from credential stuffing (covered generally in a prior paper, which specifically uses stolen username/PASSWORD pairs, not a stolen session token). A stolen session token can be obtained multiple ways — intercepting genuinely unencrypted network traffic (directly connecting to why HTTPS/TLS encryption in transit, covered generally in prior papers, matters), or via a successful XSS attack (covered earlier this paper, which can be specifically used to steal a victim''s session cookie directly from their own browser) — once obtained, the attacker simply presents that stolen token to the application, which has no inherent way to distinguish the attacker''s reuse of a valid token from the legitimate original user continuing their own already-authenticated session. This is exactly why encrypting session tokens in transit and setting genuinely short session expiration/timeout values are standard, direct mitigations — reducing both the opportunity for a token to be intercepted at all, and the useful WINDOW during which a successfully stolen token would still remain valid and exploitable even if theft does occur. This is a real, well-documented, and genuinely significant attack technique, not a scenario requiring re-authentication that a stolen token alone would always defeat.');

VALUES ('c84b5910-13e2-4940-a339-f8bf846c5541', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 5, 'An attacker compromises a company''s web server, but instead of stealing data or deploying ransomware, secretly installs software that uses the server''s own CPU resources to mine cryptocurrency for the attacker''s benefit — the company notices only an unusual, unexplained spike in CPU utilization and cloud compute costs, with no data theft or visible disruption otherwise. What is this specific unauthorized-resource-usage attack called?');
VALUES ('c84b5910-13e2-4940-a339-f8bf846c5541', '83d7aef1-8066-4f5c-96a1-73431921877c', 'Cryptojacking is specifically distinguished by its MONETIZATION mechanism — rather than encrypting the victim''s data and directly demanding a ransom payment FROM the victim (ransomware, covered generally in a prior paper, a fundamentally different and more directly disruptive/visible attack), cryptojacking instead quietly and continuously STEALS the victim''s own computing resources (CPU cycles, sometimes GPU) to perform cryptocurrency mining calculations that directly generate financial value for the attacker, without ever encrypting, exfiltrating, or otherwise directly touching the victim''s actual data at all. This genuinely different monetization model means cryptojacking''s primary observable symptoms are typically degraded system/application performance and unexpectedly elevated compute costs (particularly costly and noticeable in a cloud environment billed by actual compute usage) — rather than a ransom note, encrypted files, or evidence of data theft, meaning it can sometimes persist undetected for a genuinely longer period specifically because it doesn''t announce itself the dramatic way ransomware or an obvious data breach typically does. This is genuinely distinct from a worm (covered generally in a prior paper, defined by its self-PROPAGATION mechanism, an entirely separate characteristic from what a piece of malware actually DOES once it has spread) — cryptojacking is a real, well-documented, and genuinely common attack specifically because compromised compute resources at scale represent real, directly monetizable value to an attacker, not merely a theoretical possibility.');

VALUES ('b6458898-7da7-40e8-85d4-5a483f7c00c0', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 6, 'A security team building an insider threat program (covered generally in a prior paper) recognizes that not every insider threat looks the same: one employee deliberately and knowingly steals data for personal gain, a different employee accidentally exposes sensitive data through carelessness with no malicious intent at all, and a third employee''s otherwise-legitimate account is being controlled by an external attacker who compromised their credentials. What are these three distinct insider threat sub-types called, and why does the distinction matter for an effective response?');
VALUES ('b6458898-7da7-40e8-85d4-5a483f7c00c0', 'c1f486fe-b038-4be7-91a8-d9174d1bb444', 'This refines the general insider threat concept (covered generally in a prior paper, which focused on the deliberate, malicious example of a disgruntled employee) with the genuinely important, directly testable sub-type distinction based on the actual UNDERLYING CAUSE and intent — a malicious insider deliberately and knowingly causes harm (the classic "disgruntled employee" scenario originally used to introduce the broader concept), warranting behavioral monitoring, investigation, and likely legal/HR involvement as the appropriate response. A negligent (or unintentional) insider causes real harm through genuine carelessness or a simple mistake — no actual malicious intent exists at all — meaning the appropriate response centers on security awareness training and improving the underlying process or control that allowed the mistake to actually cause harm, not punitive action against someone who made an honest error. A compromised insider is genuinely different again — the human account holder may be entirely unaware their own credentials are being misused by an external attacker, meaning the actual appropriate response is credential-compromise incident response (revoking/resetting the compromised credential, investigating the external attacker''s activity) rather than treating the legitimate account holder themselves as the threat. Recognizing which sub-type is actually occurring genuinely matters because applying the wrong response — treating a negligent mistake punitively, or treating a compromised-account victim as a malicious actor — is both unfair and less effective than correctly matching the response to what ACTUALLY happened, which is directly why this three-way distinction is a real, standard, and practically important part of a mature insider threat program.');

-- ── Domain: Security Architecture (18% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('da4232d7-ce93-44a2-8942-5cba1709df79', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 7, 'A company implementing Zero Trust (covered generally in a prior paper, "never trust, always verify") wants to apply that principle systematically across every distinct dimension of their environment, not just network access alone. What are the distinct PILLARS a comprehensive Zero Trust architecture typically addresses?');
VALUES ('da4232d7-ce93-44a2-8942-5cba1709df79', '9c7ded3a-4950-46a3-ba83-8dcc6f8f5425', 'This directly expands the general Zero Trust principle (covered generally in a prior paper, via the "never trust, always verify" framing) into its full, structured, multi-pillar architecture — genuinely important because a Zero Trust approach applied ONLY at the network layer (verifying a user once when they connect, then implicitly trusting everything afterward) would actually reproduce much of the same "trust once, then assume safe" weakness Zero Trust exists specifically to eliminate. The Identity pillar continuously verifies WHO is making a given request, tying directly to authentication concepts covered generally elsewhere. The Device pillar verifies the requesting DEVICE''s own security posture — directly connecting to NAC (covered generally in a prior paper), which specifically evaluates device compliance before granting network access. The Network/Environment pillar segments and controls network paths — directly connecting to micro-segmentation (covered generally in a prior paper), applying continuous scrutiny to internal, East-West traffic (covered generally in a prior paper), not just the external perimeter. The Application/Workload pillar secures applications themselves, independent of the network path used to reach them. And the Data pillar protects the actual data directly — through encryption, classification, and DLP (all covered generally elsewhere) — reflecting that even a fully verified identity, on a fully compliant device, over a fully segmented network, should still not have unrestricted access to data beyond what''s genuinely necessary. This is a real, standard, and structured architectural framework (commonly associated with formal guidance like NIST SP 800-207), not merely a marketing buzzword or a single-dimension network-only concept.');

VALUES ('c5966e31-7cb1-406b-af77-348a984a94fb', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 8, 'A company wants a dedicated technology specifically inspecting ALL inbound and outbound email for phishing indicators, malicious attachments, and spam BEFORE that email ever reaches an employee''s actual inbox — a more specialized, purpose-built defense than relying solely on general employee security awareness training (covered generally in a prior paper) to catch every phishing attempt manually. What is this dedicated email-security technology called?');
VALUES ('c5966e31-7cb1-406b-af77-348a984a94fb', '50d68361-ee96-4bfa-adc4-69ad9f2bd658', 'A Secure Email Gateway is specifically the dedicated, purpose-built TECHNICAL control layer for filtering email content itself, directly complementing rather than replacing security awareness training (covered generally in a prior paper, including its phishing-simulation form) — training addresses the HUMAN judgment layer, helping employees correctly recognize and appropriately respond to whatever phishing/malicious content DOES actually reach them, while a SEG addresses the TECHNICAL filtering layer, directly reducing how much malicious email volume ever reaches an inbox in the first place by inspecting content, links, and attachments against known threat indicators before delivery. This is exactly the kind of layered, complementary defense-in-depth (covered generally in a prior paper) combination the exam specifically tests — neither purely technical filtering nor purely human training alone provides complete protection against email-based threats like phishing, whaling, and BEC (all covered generally in prior papers), which is exactly why mature security programs deploy BOTH together rather than relying on either exclusively. A CASB (covered generally in a prior paper) addresses cloud SERVICE usage policy broadly, and NAC (covered generally in a prior paper) evaluates DEVICE posture before granting network access — both genuinely different, purpose-built controls from email-content-specific filtering, which is a real, standard, and widely-deployed technology, not a gap requiring training alone to address.');

VALUES ('3cd679ce-2079-42e8-883f-dad08cc36087', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 9, 'A company gives remote employees access to a centrally-hosted, virtualized desktop environment running in the company''s own data center — employees interact with the desktop remotely, but no actual company data or applications are ever stored or run directly on the employee''s own physical device, which functions essentially as just a display/input terminal. What is this architecture called, and what specific security benefit does it provide over data and applications living directly on each employee''s own physical device?');
VALUES ('3cd679ce-2079-42e8-883f-dad08cc36087', '34ca928a-d502-451b-9330-62066fed4068', 'VDI provides a genuinely real, specific security benefit through CENTRALIZATION — since the actual company data and applications never physically leave the centrally-hosted, centrally-controlled and centrally-secured data center environment (the employee''s local device is functionally reduced to essentially a display/input terminal, with no meaningful company data ever actually stored or processed locally on it), a lost, stolen, or locally-malware-infected employee device carries meaningfully LOWER real data-exposure risk than it would if that same device had been directly storing and processing sensitive company data itself — there''s simply much less sensitive data actually AT RISK sitting on the physical device to begin with. This directly connects to and meaningfully reduces the real security tension inherent in BYOD (covered generally in a prior paper, where the organization has genuinely limited ability to enforce strong security controls on an employee''s own personally-owned device) — VDI sidesteps much of that specific tension, since even a completely unmanaged, poorly-secured personal device accessing a VDI environment is still only ever displaying and interacting with data that remains centrally hosted and controlled, rather than actually storing that sensitive data locally on the device itself. This is a real, standard, and genuinely meaningful architectural security benefit, entirely distinct from a CASB (covered generally in a prior paper, cloud-SERVICE policy enforcement) or a honeynet (covered generally in a prior paper, a deliberate decoy environment for attackers, the functional opposite of a legitimate employee-facing production system).');

VALUES ('7690eaf1-1b77-41f7-b8ae-8bb22be54fc0', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 10, 'A company wants customer service representatives to be able to VIEW a customer''s credit card number on screen for verification purposes, but only see the last four digits displayed (e.g., "**** **** **** 1234"), with the full number never actually displayed or retrievable through that specific interface at all — genuinely distinct from tokenization (covered generally in a prior paper), which replaces the real value entirely with a separately-stored substitute for later reversible lookup. What is this specific display-obscuring technique called?');
VALUES ('7690eaf1-1b77-41f7-b8ae-8bb22be54fc0', '259b3a57-8807-42d2-9572-62040a7a571b', 'Data masking is genuinely distinguished from tokenization (covered generally in a prior paper) by PURPOSE and MECHANISM — tokenization creates a separate, distinct SUBSTITUTE value (a token) with no mathematical relationship to the original, specifically for use as a stand-in value across OTHER systems, with a separate secure vault enabling authorized reversal back to the real value when genuinely needed elsewhere. Data masking instead operates specifically at the DISPLAY/presentation layer — showing a partial, deliberately obscured version of the real value (last four digits visible, everything else replaced with asterisks) to a specific VIEWER in a specific context, precisely calibrated to let that viewer accomplish their legitimate task (verifying which card a customer is asking about) without ever actually seeing the complete, full sensitive value through that particular interface — the full, real value may well continue existing entirely unmasked in the backend database, simply not exposed through THIS specific masked view. This directly connects to and complements the encryption granularity concept (covered generally in a prior paper, full-disk/file/field-level protection) with a genuinely different, VIEW-layer protection mechanism operating on top of however the underlying data is otherwise stored or protected — a real, standard, and commonly deployed technique specifically for balancing a legitimate business need to reference sensitive data against minimizing how many people and systems actually SEE the complete, unmasked value unnecessarily.');

-- ── Domain: Security Operations (28% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('086b02d0-2629-4b22-b4d6-a238c10cbee7', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 11, 'Two different companies each subscribe to threat intelligence feeds (covered generally in a prior paper) from different vendors, and want those feeds'' IOCs to be automatically shareable and consumable by their SIEM tools in a consistent, standardized format — rather than each vendor using its own incompatible, proprietary data format that requires custom integration work for every single feed source. What standardized format/protocol addresses this interoperability need?');
VALUES ('086b02d0-2629-4b22-b4d6-a238c10cbee7', '015314b0-75ce-4929-ae7a-00bd1dc91ad6', 'STIX/TAXII specifically standardizes and automates the practical interoperability problem the general threat intel/IOC concept (covered generally in a prior paper) doesn''t address on its own — merely knowing IOCs EXIST and are valuable doesn''t automatically make different vendors'' feeds consumable together, since without a shared standard, each vendor''s proprietary data format would require genuinely separate, custom integration effort to actually correlate against a SIEM (covered generally in a prior paper). STIX provides the standardized STRUCTURE for actually describing threat intelligence data itself (specific IOC types, attack patterns, and richer threat-actor context) in a consistent, machine-readable way regardless of source, while TAXII is the standardized TRANSPORT mechanism specifically for actually exchanging that STIX-formatted data automatically between systems — together, the two let genuinely different organizations'' and vendors'' threat intelligence be pooled, shared, and automatically consumed in one common format, rather than each integration being a separate, bespoke engineering effort. This is a real, standard, and widely-adopted framework specifically for exactly this automated-threat-intelligence-sharing need, distinct from CVSS (covered generally in a prior paper, vulnerability SEVERITY scoring, not threat-intel data format) and OCSP (covered generally in a prior paper, real-time CERTIFICATE revocation checking, an entirely unrelated protocol) — a real, meaningful gap this framework directly closes, not a manual-only requirement.');

VALUES ('1e36a480-6204-4b0a-8bd4-89c975eaaa2f', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 12, 'A digital forensics investigator responding to an active, ongoing incident on a STILL-RUNNING, powered-on system captures volatile data (running processes, network connections, RAM contents) WHILE the system continues running — distinct from a different investigation where the investigator first powers down a system and then examines its storage media afterward using a write blocker (covered generally in a prior paper). What are these two distinct forensic acquisition approaches called, and what does each capture that the other cannot?');
VALUES ('1e36a480-6204-4b0a-8bd4-89c975eaaa2f', '0a22f172-1ceb-4000-9c4b-88fccc1a7a3c', 'This refines the write blocker and order-of-volatility concepts (both covered generally in prior papers) with the genuinely important, directly testable acquisition-METHOD distinction of WHEN evidence is actually collected relative to the system''s power state — live acquisition happens while the system remains genuinely running, specifically because it''s the ONLY way to capture data that is fundamentally VOLATILE and would be irrecoverably lost the instant power is cut, directly connecting to why order of volatility prioritizes collecting exactly this kind of data (RAM contents, active network connections, running processes) first, before anything else. Dead-box (static) acquisition instead deliberately waits until the system has been powered down, then uses a write blocker to safely image the storage media without any risk of the SYSTEM''S OWN ongoing activity (if it were still running) altering the evidence during the collection process itself — genuinely safer and more complete for PERSISTENT disk data specifically, but fundamentally unable to capture volatile, running-state data that simply no longer exists by the time the dead-box approach even begins. Neither approach is strictly superior across the board — they capture genuinely different, complementary categories of evidence, which is exactly why real forensic investigations often use BOTH: a live acquisition first to capture what would otherwise be lost, followed by a proper dead-box acquisition of the storage media for the more complete, safely-imaged persistent-data picture.');

VALUES ('99d52435-5d03-4001-98d7-003284c8c6d2', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 13, 'A security team, too small to individually review every single line of code across every development team''s projects, instead identifies and trains one specifically interested developer WITHIN each development team to serve as that team''s embedded point of contact for security questions, conducting informal peer code reviews with a security lens and escalating genuinely complex issues to the central security team. What is this kind of program called?');
VALUES ('99d52435-5d03-4001-98d7-003284c8c6d2', '7f800110-7cde-4077-9dd2-3dca9f443591', 'A security champions program directly addresses a real, common practical constraint — most security teams are genuinely too small to directly, individually review every line of code across every development team''s work at real organizational scale — by instead distributing security AWARENESS and a first line of review capability directly INTO each individual team through one specifically identified, interested, and trained champion, who serves as that team''s local, embedded point of contact rather than every security question needing to route through the (limited-capacity) central security team directly. This genuinely scales security influence far more effectively than the central team alone attempting comprehensive direct review of everything, while also building genuine security OWNERSHIP and awareness culture directly within each team, rather than security being perceived purely as an external gatekeeping function that developers view as something imposed on them from outside rather than something they''re genuinely part of. This is a real, standard, and increasingly common practice specifically bridging security and development teams, genuinely distinct from a CSIRT (covered generally in a prior paper, a team ACTIVATED reactively for a significant INCIDENT, not an ongoing, embedded, proactive program) and a red team (covered generally in a prior paper, adversarial testing, not an ongoing internal advisory role) — embedding trained champions genuinely does provide real, meaningful additional coverage beyond what a small, centralized team could achieve entirely on its own.');

VALUES ('e67fab7a-3298-4d82-a535-0e2a122c5170', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 14, 'A company runs one vulnerability scan FROM WITHIN their own internal network (seeing what''s reachable from an already-inside perspective, like a compromised internal device or a malicious insider might see), and separately runs a completely different scan FROM OUTSIDE, over the public internet (seeing exactly what an anonymous external attacker on the internet could actually reach and see). What are these two distinct scanning PERSPECTIVES called, and why does a company genuinely need both, not just one?');
VALUES ('e67fab7a-3298-4d82-a535-0e2a122c5170', '57b36931-0683-44f9-a393-ceccb18240d3', 'This adds a genuinely important, directly testable PERSPECTIVE-based distinction to the general vulnerability scanning concept, complementary to (and genuinely different from) the credentialed-vs-non-credentialed (covered generally in a prior paper, a question of WHETHER the scanner has login access) and agent-based-vs-agentless (covered generally in a prior paper, a question of HOW the scan is deployed) distinctions already covered — internal-versus-external scanning is specifically about WHERE the scan originates FROM, a genuinely different dimension entirely. External scanning, launched from outside over the public internet, reveals exactly what an anonymous, unauthenticated external attacker with zero prior access would actually be able to see and reach — the organization''s genuine, real internet-facing attack surface, directly relevant to threats like an external attacker probing for an initial foothold. Internal scanning instead reveals what''s reachable from an ALREADY-INSIDE perspective — directly relevant to what a malicious or negligent insider (both covered earlier this paper) could reach, or what an external attacker who''s already achieved SOME initial foothold could subsequently reach while moving laterally, connecting directly to the East-West traffic and micro-segmentation concepts covered generally elsewhere. A company genuinely needs both perspectives because they answer meaningfully different, complementary real-world security questions — relying on only external scanning leaves the internal, post-foothold risk picture completely unassessed, and relying on only internal scanning misses understanding the organization''s actual external attack surface entirely.');

VALUES ('879f67de-65e0-48db-8bdc-ebbab4524b12', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 15, 'A mid-sized company without a large, dedicated internal incident response team signs a pre-arranged contract with an external specialized incident response firm, agreeing on response-time commitments and general terms of engagement BEFORE any actual incident ever occurs — specifically so that, when a genuine security incident eventually does happen, expert help can be engaged immediately rather than needing to find, vet, and negotiate with a firm for the first time while already in the middle of an active crisis. What is this kind of pre-arranged contractual relationship called?');
VALUES ('879f67de-65e0-48db-8bdc-ebbab4524b12', '86e8735f-01ff-4c8c-a2f8-ba69c6ba1b1e', 'An incident response retainer directly addresses a genuinely real, practical problem for organizations (particularly smaller or mid-sized ones) without the scale to justify maintaining a large, fully-staffed dedicated internal CSIRT (covered generally in a prior paper, the internal team structure activated for a significant incident) — the absolute WORST time to first start searching for, vetting, negotiating pricing and terms with, and onboarding an unfamiliar external firm is precisely when a genuine, active incident is already unfolding and every hour of delay meaningfully compounds the damage. Establishing a retainer relationship BEFORE any incident ever occurs — pre-negotiating response-time commitments (how quickly the firm will actually mobilize once called), scope, and general engagement terms in advance — means that when a real incident eventually does happen, genuinely specialized expert help can be engaged essentially immediately, with the relationship, contract terms, and expectations already fully established, rather than losing genuinely critical early response time to administrative negotiation during the crisis itself. This is a real, standard, and practically important preparedness practice, genuinely distinct from a right-to-audit clause (covered generally in a prior paper, a vendor-security-verification contract term, an entirely different purpose) — organizations absolutely CAN and regularly do pre-arrange this kind of external expert relationship well in advance of ever actually needing it.');

VALUES ('5a1b3c50-87c7-4386-bfc8-ee91f5e3173d', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 16, 'A SOC analyst investigating suspicious activity wants to classify the SPECIFIC techniques an observed attacker actually appears to be using — for example, recognizing a specific technique as matching a known, catalogued pattern for "credential dumping" or "lateral movement via remote services" — using a standardized, widely-adopted, publicly-available catalog of real-world adversary tactics and techniques observed across many actual incidents, rather than relying purely on the analyst''s own personal knowledge and experience to informally categorize what they''re seeing. What is this standardized catalog called?');
VALUES ('5a1b3c50-87c7-4386-bfc8-ee91f5e3173d', '847159e9-2b34-46c6-b7db-66c96198be53', 'MITRE ATT&CK is specifically the standardized, shared reference KNOWLEDGE BASE for classifying and communicating about real-world adversary behavior — genuinely distinct from CVSS (covered generally in a prior paper, which scores an individual VULNERABILITY''s abstract technical severity, not attacker BEHAVIOR/technique patterns) and from STIX/TAXII (covered earlier this paper, which is specifically the DATA FORMAT and TRANSPORT protocol for exchanging threat intelligence, a different, complementary purpose from ATT&CK''s role as the actual CONTENT/knowledge-base of catalogued technique names and descriptions — the two are frequently used together, with STIX/TAXII often used to actually transport threat intelligence that references specific ATT&CK technique identifiers). ATT&CK organizes real, observed adversary behavior into TACTICS (the attacker''s higher-level GOAL at a given stage of an attack, like gaining initial access, escalating privilege, or moving laterally) and, under each tactic, specific TECHNIQUES (the concrete, specific methods actually used to achieve that goal) — giving security teams a shared, standardized, and widely-recognized vocabulary rather than each individual analyst using their own inconsistent, informal terminology, directly useful both during live incident investigation (mapping observed activity to known technique patterns helps predict what an attacker might do NEXT, based on how similar attacks have typically progressed) and during the proactive threat modeling process (covered generally in a prior paper), informing which realistic techniques a design-phase security review should specifically consider. This is a real, extremely widely-adopted, and genuinely foundational industry framework, not a hypothetical or non-existent resource.');

-- ── Domain: Security Program Management & Oversight (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('d9ec0ef5-c4df-450f-a281-f45ed0a952f0', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 17, 'A company adopts the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (mentioned generally alongside ISO 27001 in a prior paper as an example security framework/standard) and wants to understand its actual internal STRUCTURE — the core organizing categories it uses to describe a comprehensive cybersecurity program. What are the NIST CSF''s core functions?');
VALUES ('d9ec0ef5-c4df-450f-a281-f45ed0a952f0', 'a71313d7-52a0-488f-b481-797c24ae28f8', 'This directly names and structures the NIST CSF (previously only mentioned generally alongside ISO 27001 as an EXAMPLE of a security framework in a prior paper, without detailing its actual internal organization) with its five real, standard core functions, which genuinely serve as an organizing lens essentially every other Security+ concept covered throughout this entire project can be mapped back to: Identify covers understanding the organization''s own assets, risk landscape, and environment (gap analysis and risk registers, both covered generally in prior papers, directly support this function). Protect covers actually implementing the wide range of specific safeguards — encryption, access control, hardening, awareness training, and the large majority of individual technical/administrative controls covered throughout this project''s content genuinely map to this single function. Detect covers actually identifying that a security event is genuinely occurring — SIEM, EDR, and threat hunting (all covered generally in prior papers) are core Detect-function capabilities. Respond covers the actual actions taken once an incident IS detected — the incident response lifecycle, playbooks, and CSIRT activation (all covered generally in prior papers) map here. And Recover covers restoring normal capabilities and services AFTER an incident — BCP/DR, backup restoration, and the various alternate-site strategies (all covered generally in prior papers) map to this final function. This is a real, standard, and widely-adopted framework structure — not an unstructured recommendation, and genuinely distinct from PCI-DSS (an industry PAYMENT-CARD-specific standard, covered generally in a prior paper) and FedRAMP (a GOVERNMENT CLOUD-authorization program, covered generally in a prior paper), neither of which is organized around these specific five core functions.');

VALUES ('5fa0116c-70db-47ec-9977-495bfc0f3817', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 18, 'A company negotiating a vendor contract includes a specific clause stating that the VENDOR will cover the company''s legal costs and damages if the vendor''s own product defect or security failure directly causes the company harm — distinct from a right-to-audit clause (covered generally in a prior paper), which instead grants the customer verification rights rather than allocating financial liability. What is this specific type of contract clause called, and why does it matter for vendor risk management?');
VALUES ('5fa0116c-70db-47ec-9977-495bfc0f3817', 'b5d85312-7e3e-47ce-8c25-73ce90839165', 'An indemnification clause is specifically distinguished from a right-to-audit clause (covered generally in a prior paper) by its PURPOSE — right-to-audit grants the customer the ability to independently VERIFY a vendor''s security practices, while indemnification instead directly allocates FINANCIAL LIABILITY for actual harm that results if the vendor''s own failure genuinely causes the customer damage, obligating the vendor to cover the customer''s resulting legal costs and damages rather than the customer bearing that full financial consequence entirely alone. This is genuinely important, practical vendor risk management (covered generally in a prior paper) leverage precisely because a vendor''s security failure can genuinely cause the CUSTOMER real financial and legal harm (regulatory fines, customer lawsuits, remediation costs) even though the underlying failure was the vendor''s own fault — without a genuine, real indemnification provision explicitly negotiated into the contract, a company harmed this way could find itself with no meaningful contractual recourse against the actual vendor responsible, left bearing the full financial consequence of someone else''s failure entirely on its own. This is a real, standard, and commonly negotiated (though sometimes contested and limited-in-scope) contract provision, genuinely distinct from an MOU (covered generally in a prior paper, a typically non-binding statement of shared understanding, not a liability-allocation mechanism) — organizations absolutely CAN and regularly do negotiate this kind of liability allocation directly into a vendor contract.');

VALUES ('d71f2d79-2eeb-4466-9a7d-4b10a0ce167a', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 19, 'A company operating a website and mobile app that is knowingly directed at and used by children under the age of 13 in the United States must comply with a specific US federal law imposing strict requirements around obtaining verifiable parental consent before collecting a child''s personal information. What is this specific regulation called, and how is it distinct from the other data protection regulations covered elsewhere?');
VALUES ('d71f2d79-2eeb-4466-9a7d-4b10a0ce167a', 'db716d39-79e9-4c86-bab7-71e253d77cd2', 'COPPA is specifically distinguished by being scoped around the AGE of the data subject — children under 13 specifically — a genuinely different organizing principle from every other regulation covered across this project''s Security+ content, each scoped instead by data TYPE or industry/subject matter: HIPAA (covered generally in a prior paper) by health information specifically, GDPR (covered generally in a prior paper) by the personal data of EU residents broadly regardless of age, PCI-DSS (covered generally in a prior paper) by payment card data specifically as an industry standard, SOX (covered generally in a prior paper) by financial reporting integrity for publicly traded companies, and FedRAMP (covered generally in a prior paper) by cloud services sold specifically to US federal government agencies. COPPA specifically requires services knowingly directed at, or that they know are actually being used by, children under 13 to obtain genuinely VERIFIABLE parental consent before collecting that child''s personal information at all, reflecting the real, distinct policy concern that children specifically cannot meaningfully consent to data collection themselves the way an adult legally can. This genuinely adds a real, meaningful additional dimension to the "which regulation applies to my organization" analysis — an organization''s actual user base/audience (specifically, whether it includes children under 13) matters just as much as what TYPE of data is being handled, a real, distinct, and directly enforced US federal requirement, not a non-existent or purely theoretical consideration.');

VALUES ('7c193617-0714-4bfb-a1d5-0ccd12063ce6', '0634cd42-b318-4742-a8c4-77d3a8f31ca2', 20, 'A company is preparing to ACQUIRE a smaller competitor and specifically wants its security team to assess the target company''s actual security posture — existing vulnerabilities, past breach history, compliance gaps, and technical debt — BEFORE the acquisition deal is finalized, rather than only discovering these issues AFTER the acquisition has already closed and those problems have effectively become the acquiring company''s own. What is this specific security assessment practice called, and why does timing matter so much here?');
VALUES ('7c193617-0714-4bfb-a1d5-0ccd12063ce6', '956bdb57-4c0e-4488-8388-b316af69964b', 'M&A security due diligence is specifically the application of the general due diligence principle (covered generally in a prior paper — reasonable, pre-decision INVESTIGATION before an action, there applied to vendor selection) to the specific, high-stakes context of a company acquisition — and the TIMING here matters enormously, genuinely more than in many other due diligence contexts, precisely because an acquisition is typically a single, largely irreversible transaction: a security issue genuinely discovered BEFORE the deal closes can still meaningfully influence the outcome — the purchase price can be adjusted downward to reflect real remediation costs the acquirer will now bear, specific remediation can be contractually required as an explicit condition of closing, or in a sufficiently severe case (a genuinely catastrophic, unaddressed vulnerability, or an undisclosed prior breach), the acquiring company can simply walk away from the deal entirely. Once the acquisition has already closed, essentially none of those options remain realistically available — the target company''s pre-existing security debt, vulnerabilities, and compliance gaps become directly and unavoidably the acquirer''s OWN problem to now deal with, often only becoming fully apparent well after the deal has already closed and the leverage to negotiate around them has already been lost. This is a real, standard, and genuinely important practice specifically involving the security team directly in M&A transactions (not left purely to financial and legal due diligence alone) — technology and security risk are real, material factors in evaluating whether and at what price an acquisition genuinely makes sense.');
